**Read:** [Petrology — Stephen A. Nelson, Tulane EENS 2120](../sources/Petrology-Nelson-Tulane.lingua#r_1MNEKH4WW2GQRD4FZWNRD1GDPZ), the complete fluid/redox and metamorphic-reaction notes used; [HPx-eos and THERMOCALC — models, data, and interpretation](../sources/HPx-eos-and-THERMOCALC.lingua#r_0QYHHGNWRA8RQEM5MADWXDRQFJ), the full fluid/endmember/model definitions for the selected system; [Perple_X — Connolly, phase-equilibrium methods and examples](../sources/Perple-X-Methods-and-Documentation.lingua#r_39E8B7CK16VF55MXWKSXMNN4Z6), the full relevant component, constraint, and data-model documentation.

Fluids can transfer components, change chemical potentials, enable reactions, and affect mineral stability and kinetics. Water saturation, a fixed bulk water amount, and an imposed fluid chemical potential are different constraints. A rock containing hydrous minerals is not automatically saturated with an abundant equilibrium fluid throughout its history.

Oxygen fugacity expresses a chemical-potential account under a reference convention; it is not simply the measured oxygen weight percent. Iron valence, buffers, fluids, sulfur/carbon species, and mineral compositions can constrain redox under specific models and conditions. Preserve which quantities are measured and which are imposed or inferred.

Closed-system, externally buffered, and infiltrating/reactive-transport accounts can predict different assemblages even with similar initial rock names. A phase calculation may assume a fluid composition that the specimen never experienced. Textures, veins, reaction fronts, and mass transfer provide geological tests of the boundary assumptions.

**Work:** compare a dry/finite-water and a fluid-buffered calculation for an identified teaching system, deriving the component/constraint differences. Explain how a ferric-iron or CO₂ assumption could alter a result. Design an observation or analysis that would discriminate the competing fluid/redox accounts.

Read the complete thermodynamic/fluid notes and method/data documentation. Later metamorphic, hydrothermal, and ore/feedstock studies reuse these constraints with their own evidence rather than imposing one convenient default on every natural material.

